WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.259
OK, pop quiz.

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:04.419
What am I drawing?

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.489
I'm going to make three

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.079
predictions here.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.080
Firstly.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.369
You think at your house, you'd be

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:14.419
right?

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.429
Secondly, that that

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:18.019
just came pretty easily to you, it

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:18.799
was effortless.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.719
And thirdly, you're thinking

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.999
that I'm not much of an artist

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.709
and you'd be right on all counts

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:25.519
there.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.469
But how can we look at this set

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.779
of geometric shapes and think,

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.879
Oh, how?

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.229
If you live in a house, I bet it

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.549
looks nothing like this.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.739
Well, that ability to perform

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.299
object identification that comes so

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.459
easily to us does not

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.889
come so easily to a computer,

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:44.839
but that is where we can apply

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.449
something called convolutional

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.549
neural networks

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:50.550
to the problem.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.329
Now, a convolutional neural

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.629
network or a.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:57.890
See, and and.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.189
Is a area of deep learning

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:02.659
that specializes in pattern

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:03.660
recognition.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.189
My name is Martin Keane, and

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.109
I work in the IBM garage

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.609
at IBM.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.469
Now let's take a look

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.569
at how CNN works

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.189
at a high level.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:17.839
Well, let's break it down.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.149
CNN convolutional neural network

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:21.919
Well, let's start with the

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.529
artificial neural network part.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:26.419
This is a standard network

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.429
that consists of multiple layers

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.529
that are interconnected,

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.569
and each layer receives

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.299
some input.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.039
Transforms that input to something

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.289
else and passes an output

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.449
to the next layer, that's

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.519
how neural networks work and

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.379
see an end is a particular

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.329
part of the neural network or a

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:47.859
section of layers that say it's

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.619
these three layers here

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.299
and within these layers, we have

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.209
something called filters.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:58.299
And it's the filters that perform

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.909
the pattern recognition

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:02.919
that CNN is so good

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:03.999
at.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.099
So let's apply

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.109
this to our house example now.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.629
If this house were an actual image,

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.639
it would be a series

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.740
of pixels, just like any image.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.349
And if we zoom in on a particular

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.479
part of this house,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.609
let's say we zoom in around here,

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.369
then we would get, well,

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.370
the window.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.489
And what we're saying here is that a

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:32.679
window consists of some

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:33.909
perfectly straight lines.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.459
Almost perfectly straight lines.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:38.659
But, you know, a window doesn't need

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.069
to look like that window could

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.869
equally look like this, and we would

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.299
still say it was a window.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.339
The cool thing about CNN is

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:47.899
that using filters.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.729
CNN could also say that these

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:51.709
two objects represent the same

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:52.819
thing.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.289
The way they do that, then, is

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.759
through the application of these

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.529
filters. So let's take a look at how

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:58.729
that works.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:01.399
Now, a filter is basically

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.279
just a three by three block.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:05.869
And within that block, we can

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.659
specify a pattern to look for.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.639
So we could say, let's look

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:11.640
for.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.429
Pattern like this, a right

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.319
angle in our

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:17.339
image.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.469
So what we do is we take this filter

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.119
and it's a three by three block

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.009
here. We will analyze the equivalent

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.839
three by three block up here as

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:25.619
well.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:26.620
So.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.749
We'll look at first of all, these

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:29.750
first.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:32.279
Group of three by three pixels,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.199
and we will see how close

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.089
are they to the filter

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.090
shape?

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.709
And we'll get that numeric score,

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.549
then we will move across one, come

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:44.039
to the right and look at the next

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:45.779
three by three block of pixels and

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:47.279
score how close they are to the

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:48.239
filter shape.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.099
And we will continue to slide over

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.169
or vote over all

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.569
of these pixel layers until

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:57.029
we have not every

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.110
three by three block.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.829
Now, that's just for one filter.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.329
But what that will give us is an

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:04.889
array of numbers that say how

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.959
closely and the image

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.149
matches filter,

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.039
but we can add more filters

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.809
so we could add another three by

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:13.979
three filter here.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.719
And perhaps this one looks for a

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.429
shape like this.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:20.708
And we could add a third filter

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:22.659
here, and perhaps this looks

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.459
for a different kind of right angle

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:25.460
shape.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.709
If we take the numeric arrays

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:31.059
from all of these filters and

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.499
combine them together in a process

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.449
called pooling, then we have

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:36.369
a much better understanding

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:38.499
of what is contained within

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.749
this series of pixels.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.699
Now that's just the first layer

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:43.989
of the CNN.

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:45.909
And as we go deeper into the

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:47.769
neural network, the filters

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.689
become more abstract all they can do

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:51.039
more.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.019
So the second

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:55.089
layer of filters perhaps can perform

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.919
tasks like basic object

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:58.239
recognition.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.069
So we can have filters here that

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:02.259
might be able to recognize

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.659
the presence of a window

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:06.609
or the presence of a door

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:08.499
or the presence

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:09.500
of a roof.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.349
And as we go deeper into the sea

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:13.729
and into the next leg, well, maybe

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:16.159
these filters can perform even more

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.139
abstract tasks, like

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.999
being able to determine whether

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.669
we're looking at a house

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.579
or we're looking at an apartment

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:26.899
or whether we're looking at a

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:27.900
skyscraper.

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:31.839
So you can see the application

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.419
of these filters increases

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:35.949
as we go through the network and can

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:37.719
perform more and more tasks.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:39.849
And that's a very high level

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:42.039
basic overview of what CNN

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.229
is. It has a ton of business

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:45.219
applications.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.169
Think of OCR, for example,

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:48.579
for understanding handwritten

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:49.839
documents.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.669
Think of visual recognition

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:53.649
and facial detection and visual

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:54.819
search.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:56.709
Think of medical imagery and

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:58.629
looking at that and determining what

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:01.059
is being shown in an imaging scan.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.979
And even think of the fact that

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:05.049
we can apply a CNN to perform

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:06.910
object identification for.

